    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good high-level summary. To get more granular for our search committee, can you expand on the specific behavioral cues? \n\nI'm thinking about things like vocal tone, body language, and even how they carry themselves in social/networking settings. What are the specific markers that signal \"gravitas\" vs someone who just talks a lot? I want to be able to tell my clients exactly what a person should 'look and sound like' when they possess that top-tier leadership profile.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Gravitas is signaled by controlled presence rather than frantic energy, both verbally and physically. Look for a deliberate, measured pace in their speech with a lower, resonant vocal tone that conveys thoughtfulness. Body language should be grounded: confident posture without stiffness, deliberate gestures, and maintaining calm, direct eye contact. In networking, a gravitas-driven leader listens deeply before contributing, ensuring every comment is impactful and adds strategic value to the conversation.",
      "source": "target",
